From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 12:31:36 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio_balloon: do not wait for an answer from a broken
 device

tell_host() ignored the result of virtqueue_add_outbuf() and then waited,
uninterruptibly, for the host to return the buffer. A broken virtqueue
never returns one: the add fails with -EIO, and a buffer queued before
the break stays out, because virtqueue_get_buf() returns nothing once
the queue is broken and its interrupt no longer runs the callback.

UML reaches that state on every power-off with an inflated balloon: it
runs virtio_uml_exit() as an exitcall after device_shutdown() has broken
and reset every virtio device, and virtio_balloon's remove then returns
its pages through tell_host(). The guest stopped for ever after
"reboot: Power down". The .shutdown handler 6.18.52 added quiesces the
balloon's workers before that break but leaves those pages in the
balloon, so remove still reaches tell_host() on the broken queue.

That handler also leaves balloon page migration running. A compaction
that migrates a balloon page while the queues break waits in
tell_host() for ever, holding balloon_lock, and remove then waits for
that lock: an Android guest that compacts as it powers off sometimes
never exits.

Return when the buffer cannot be queued, and stop waiting when the
queue breaks under the request, as a failed kick breaks it. Hold
balloon_lock while the shutdown handler breaks the queues, so that a
migration telling the host finishes first and one that starts later
finds them broken. The pages still leave the balloon; a device that is
gone cannot be told.
---
 drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 23 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c b/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
index a3995e77a652ca3a750bd2799243cc052dad7ba2..2f9e5b0dcfa0dd58dd674dde72173f09f938b8c3 100644
--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_balloon.c
@@ -188,12 +188,20 @@ static void tell_host(struct virtio_balloon *vb, struct virtqueue *vq)
 
 	sg_init_one(&sg, vb->pfns, sizeof(vb->pfns[0]) * vb->num_pfns);
 
-	/* We should always be able to add one buffer to an empty queue. */
-	virtqueue_add_outbuf(vq, &sg, 1, vb, GFP_KERNEL);
+	/*
+	 * We should always be able to add one buffer to an empty queue, unless
+	 * the device is broken, and then no answer will ever come.
+	 */
+	if (virtqueue_add_outbuf(vq, &sg, 1, vb, GFP_KERNEL))
+		return;
 	virtqueue_kick(vq);
 
-	/* When host has read buffer, this completes via balloon_ack */
-	wait_event(vb->acked, virtqueue_get_buf(vq, &len));
+	/*
+	 * When host has read buffer, this completes via balloon_ack. A queue
+	 * that breaks meanwhile, as a failed kick breaks it, returns nothing.
+	 */
+	wait_event(vb->acked,
+		   virtqueue_get_buf(vq, &len) || virtqueue_is_broken(vq));
 
 }
 
@@ -1157,8 +1165,18 @@ static void virtballoon_remove(struct virtio_device *vdev)
 
 static void virtballoon_shutdown(struct virtio_device *vdev)
 {
-	virtballoon_quiesce(vdev->priv);
+	struct virtio_balloon *vb = vdev->priv;
+
+	virtballoon_quiesce(vb);
+	/*
+	 * Quiescing leaves balloon page migration running. Let a migration
+	 * that is telling the host finish before the queues break, because a
+	 * broken queue never returns its buffer; one that starts later finds
+	 * the queues broken and does not wait.
+	 */
+	mutex_lock(&vb->balloon_lock);
 	virtio_device_shutdown(vdev);
+	mutex_unlock(&vb->balloon_lock);
 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_PM_SLEEP
